12
00:51:36,000 --> 00:51:42,000
The Krampus (German: [ˈkʁampʊs]) is a horned anthropomorphic figure who, in the Central and Eastern Alpine folkloric tradition, is said to accompany
Saint Nicholas on visits to children during the night of 5 December (Krampusnacht; "Krampus Night"), immediately before the Feast of St.

13
00:55:54,000 --> 00:56:00,000
Nicholas on 6 December.

14
01:00:12,000 --> 01:00:18,000
In this tradition, Saint Nicholas rewards well-behaved children with small
gifts, while Krampus punishes badly behaved ones with birch rods.

15
01:04:30,000 --> 01:04:36,000
The origin of the figure is unclear; some folklorists and anthropologists have postulated that it may have pre-Christian
origins, although historians generally consider this unlikely, as it is not attested until the 16th century.

16
01:08:48,000 --> 01:08:54,000
In certain traditional parades and in such events as the Krampuslauf ("Krampus run"), some
young men dressed as Krampus attempt to scare the audience with their antics.

17
01:13:06,000 --> 01:13:12,000
Krampus is featured on holiday greeting cards called Krampuskarten.

18
01:17:24,000 --> 01:17:30,000
The figure has been imported into popular culture around the world,
and has appeared in movies, TV shows and video games.

19
01:21:42,000 --> 01:21:48,000
The tone is dark.
